{"id":"v-earnings-fiscal-q4-2026","title":"V earnings — Visa, fiscal Q4 2026","section":"Earnings","date":"2026-10-27","html":"<p>Management sees a resilient consumer and another year of double-digit growth, a little slower than the last, and the thing that could spoil it is pressure on card fees, from the banks it must pay to keep and from courts and Congress.</p>\n<p>Reported Oct 27, 2026 (quarter ended Sept 30). Next report: not found (last year's fiscal Q1 report was Jan 29, per a search snippet only). Updated Nov 12, 2026. Research, not investment advice.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-short-version\">The short version</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Demand:</strong> steady. US payments volume +10% in the quarter and +10% again in October through the 21st; cross-border +14%.</li><li><strong>Guidance:</strong> first outlook for fiscal 2027, so nothing to compare. Revenue growth in the low double digits and EPS growth in the low double digits to low teens, against fiscal 2026's actual +15% and +16%. That is a slowdown.</li><li><strong>Biggest worry:</strong> pressure on card fees. About 25% of payments volume is up for renewal in fiscal 2027, and the merchant settlement goes to a final approval hearing on Nov 16.</li><li><strong>Chart:</strong> leans up. The level that decides it is your 2.382 ext at $423.52 on a weekly close; your 2.618 ext at $442.75 is the ceiling.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-numbers\">The numbers</h3>\n<ul><li>Net revenue $12.14B against about $12.09B expected. A small beat.</li><li>Adjusted EPS $3.48 against $3.43 (Zacks). A beat. GAAP EPS was $3.19, which Webull sets against a $3.38 estimate and shows as a miss; that comparison mixes GAAP with an adjusted estimate.</li><li>Client incentives in the quarter were $5.1B, up 21% on the year, growing faster than revenue. The litigation provision was $474M.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-said-about-the-road-ahead\">What management said about the road ahead</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Next quarter:</strong> revenue growth in the low double digits; EPS growth at the low end of the mid-teens.</li><li><strong>Full year:</strong> operating expenses to grow slightly slower than revenue.</li><li><strong>Consumer:</strong> no weakening among lower-spending cardholders; growth was even across spending bands and fastest at the top. &quot;We did not see signs of the lower-spend consumer weakening in our volumes.&quot; Chris Suh, CFO.</li><li><strong>Assumption:</strong> the economy stays about where it is. Management credits jobs and real wage growth.</li><li><strong>Incentives:</strong> renewals rise to about 25% of payments volume from about 20%, so incentive growth speeds up.</li><li><strong>Cross-border:</strong> the Middle East conflict brings near-term uncertainty for travel in that region.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-is-worried-about\">What management is worried about</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Rising client incentives.</strong> $18.3B in fiscal 2026 against $15.8B the year before (10-K), with a heavier renewal year ahead.</li><li><strong>US fee rules and lawsuits.</strong> The 10-K names the Credit Card Competition Act, possible lower debit interchange caps, the Justice Department's debit suit and the merchant litigation. Litigation provision: $1.76B in fiscal 2026.</li><li><strong>Middle East and cross-border travel.</strong> Cross-border volume grew 14%; no figure given for the region.</li><li><strong>New ways to pay.</strong> Stablecoins, account-to-account payments and AI shopping agents are all named in the 10-K.</li><li><strong>Currency swings and AI-enabled cyber risk.</strong></li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"the-big-worry-pressure-on-card-fees-both-ways\">The big worry: pressure on card fees, both ways</h3>\n<p>One thing to keep straight: interchange is paid by merchants to card-issuing banks, not to Visa. Visa earns its own network fees. But lower interchange squeezes the banks, and the banks then press Visa for bigger incentives or look at rival networks.</p>\n<p><strong>What would ease it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Final approval of the merchant settlement. The hearing is Nov 16, 2026. It cuts credit interchange by 0.1 percentage point for five years and caps the standard consumer rate at 1.25% for eight years, which sets the terms for years. The court upheld its forward-looking release on Sept 29.</li><li>The Credit Card Competition Act staying stalled. As of August it had no committee markup, floor vote or bill to ride on, and the Senate passed its housing bill in March without it.</li><li>The White House had backed away from a credit card interest-rate cap by the end of October (Bloomberg, via PYMNTS).</li><li>Time on the Justice Department's debit suit: no trial date, with trial likely in 2028 (Payments Dive).</li><li>Opt-out merchants are mostly settled: about 96% of opt-out Visa sales volume (10-K).</li></ul>\n<p><strong>What would make it worse</strong></p>\n<ul><li>The court rejecting the settlement. The National Retail Federation and Walmart are among the objectors, and three merchants appealed on Oct 1.</li><li>A new Congress. Democrats swept the midterms (Trading Economics), candidates of both parties campaigned on rate caps, and the President backed the Competition Act in January. Visa's own 10-K says it is unclear whether the bill advances.</li><li>Debit fees. On Oct 26 an appeals court upheld a ruling that throws out the Fed's 21-cent debit cap; it is on hold for now, and the Fed has a lower 14.4-cent proposal that is not adopted.</li><li>Incentives rising faster than volume. Mastercard's rebates and incentives grew 18% last quarter and its CFO expects the share to rise again in Q4.</li><li>Europe: the Commission opened a preliminary investigation into Visa's fees to acquirers in August 2024.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>How this company is placed against it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Incentives already take 28.5% of gross revenue ($18.3B of $64.2B, my sum of the 10-K revenue lines), up from 28.3%. The trend is slow, not sudden.</li><li>Revenue still grew 15% in fiscal 2026 while paying those incentives, and the fiscal 2027 outlook already builds in the heavier renewal year.</li><li>Cash is not the constraint: $20.5B of buybacks in the year, $24.4B still authorised, and $1.3B went into the US litigation escrow.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"money-plans\">Money plans</h3>\n<ul><li>Buybacks: $4.0B in the quarter, $20.5B in the year, $24.4B authorised.</li><li>Dividend raised 16% to $0.78 a quarter.</li><li>Buying BioCatch for $2.4B.</li><li>Capital spending and total debt: not found.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"people-and-governance\">People and governance</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>CEO:</strong> Ryan McInerney, since February 2023. Not a founder; a hired chief executive.</li><li><strong>CFO:</strong> Chris Suh, since August 2023.</li><li><strong>Successor named:</strong> not found. The proxy says a board committee reviews succession plans.</li><li><strong>Control:</strong> no controlling holder. No one may own more than 15%. The class B and C shares held by banks vote only on a few matters, such as leaving the core payments business or certain mergers. Largest holders: Vanguard about 8.2%, BlackRock about 7.3%.</li><li><strong>Chair:</strong> independent, John Lundgren, since January 2024.</li><li><strong>Recent changes or investigations:</strong> no leadership change found in the last 12 months. The open matters are against the company, not individuals: the Justice Department's debit suit and the European Commission inquiry.</li><li><strong>How much rests on one person:</strong> very little. This is a network business with a split chair and no founder.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"does-the-chart-agree\">Does the chart agree?</h3>\n<figure class=\"chart\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" data-ticker=\"V\" viewBox=\"0 0 760 400\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"V · daily · your extension levels chart\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\"><text x=\"8\" y=\"18\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#20242C\" class=\"c-ink\">V · daily · yo